# In-game MCP sessions and match control

## Controls added after live testing

The session now includes native menu navigation, deck and card selection popups,
either-seat deck replacement, per-card battle-stat overrides and explicit damage
application. The false pending-effect error from the solo playtest is fixed:
an inert previous attack lifecycle is no longer treated as an unresolved effect.
Real choices, queued effects and armed attack rollback still block ordinary edits.

Open **Settings → MCP session → Browse cards and decks** to browse either seat.
A selected deck or card list is returned to the agent; selecting alone never
replaces or removes anything. The agent applies the requested action explicitly.
Multiplayer requires the existing mutual human approval; both peers must use the
same updated build. A real two-computer internet acceptance test is still pending.

The full Windows game includes opt-in match control for reproducible
testing. The local MCP server exposes 34 tools so Codex can prepare cards and
positions from ordinary requests. It uses the official MCP TypeScript SDK over
stdio; a local file request queue connects it to Unity's main thread. No hosted
service, network listener or model API key is required.

## Start a session from Settings

Launch the game normally through `./Play-Unity.ps1`, Steam, or
`builds/ModernWindows/PokemonCardTable.exe`. Open the Settings gear and choose
**MCP session** in **Explore & connect**. No development launcher, launch flag or
special build is required.

In solo/local play, press **Start MCP session**. This enables access to the
current game and pauses solo AI. If a match is in progress, the game saves an
automatic `session-start-…` checkpoint and makes that match practice-only.
Matches started while MCP access is enabled are also practice-only. The game
continues using the same profile, deck lists and asset caches. The agent can
create a seeded position or restore a named checkpoint directly from Home.

The session panel provides **Copy connection command** and **Copy session
folder**, pause/resume, other-seat inspection/takeover, and **Stop MCP session**.
Stopping disables agent access and resumes solo AI; the current test position
stays on the table and remains practice-only. Restore a checkpoint before
stopping to discard an experiment.

The local session directory is `%LOCALAPPDATA%/PokemonTCG/McpSession`. Only one
game process can own it at a time. Closing the game removes its active manifest;
named checkpoints stay available next time. The game rotates the session
identity when starting again or approving a new multiplayer request, so queued
commands cannot borrow a later approval.

## Connect an agent once

The in-game button starts the Unity control session. The agent's MCP client
automatically launches the stdio adapter when connecting; it is not a separate
game command or an independently running console server. Node.js must be on the
agent machine's PATH. Installed Windows players include the adapter and its
dependencies in the `MCP` folder beside the executable.

In the session panel, **Copy connection command** produces an absolute command
for that installed game and session. Run that command once to register it in
Codex. For this repository, the equivalent is:

```powershell
codex mcp add pokemon-tcg-dev -- node "C:\Users\OEM\Documents\GitHub\pokemon TCG\tools\Tcg.DevMcp\server.mjs"
codex mcp get pokemon-tcg-dev
```

For a fresh source checkout, first run
`npm ci --prefix tools/Tcg.DevMcp --ignore-scripts`. Adjust the absolute path when
moving the repository. If needed, reload MCP
connections in Codex after registration. The server starts without the game;
calls explain how to start a session when no running player is available.
An optional `--session <absolute-directory>` server argument selects another
test session. For another MCP client, use command `node`, arguments containing
the absolute `server.mjs` path, transport `stdio`, and optionally `--session`
followed by the absolute session directory. No API key or port is needed.

## Multiplayer approval and takeover

Both players run the same current game build with Steam.
Find an opponent and choose decks using the ordinary lobby flow.

Either the host or guest can open **Settings → MCP session** in the lobby or
battlefield and press **Request opponent approval**.
The other player receives an explicit approval prompt covering inspection of
hidden hands, decks and Prizes, control of either seat, edits, checkpoints and
ChatGPT tool access. A requester cannot approve their own request. Declining
keeps all development access disabled.

Approval starts shared testing paused and clears both lobby Ready flags. If
approved in the lobby, both players still choose decks and ready up to start.
The **MCP session** panel offers opponent-hand inspection/takeover, return to the
normal seat, pause/resume, and stop. Inspection/takeover switches the battlefield
to that seat's hand and legal controls. MCP `get_state` exposes both sides only
while mutual approval is active; `set_viewer` selects either viewing/control
seat, and `dispatch` can act for either seat.

The host validates and applies edits; guest tools forward requests to that same
authority. Complete states synchronize to both clients. Consent epochs and
shared revisions reject expired approvals and stale edits. Either player can
revoke access; leaving/disconnection clears consent and cached full states.
Reconnecting requires a new request and approval. Stopping development mode
retains the current position; restore a checkpoint first to discard an experiment.
Previously authorized saved checkpoints remain stored in the development folder.

The shared match stays inside the existing lobby and recipient-view protocol.
Normal game actions remain filtered to each player when takeover is off. Saved
deck lists, collection data and wallets are not edited by these match controls.

## Requests and tools

Examples:

- “Give me two Nest Balls and a Fire Energy, with an empty Bench.”
- “Put this Pokémon into the Active Spot, attach three Energy and leave it at 20 HP.”
- “Save this position as before-retreat, then test the retreat and go back.”
- “Show me the other player's hand and take over their next action.”
- “Roll back one recorded turn and capture the battlefield.”
- “Inspect the pending choice and work out which legal actions are available.”

| Tools | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `get_capabilities`, `get_session` | Agent workflow guide and compact session/board status |
| `get_state`, `get_card`, `search_cards`, `list_decks` | Complete state, targeted effective card stats, supported printings and built-in/saved deck lists |
| `get_ui`, `open_menu` | Current screens, available menus, button state and navigation |
| `open_deck_picker`, `get_deck_selection`, `replace_deck` | Human deck selection and explicit replacement of either seat |
| `open_card_picker`, `get_card_selection`, `move_cards` | Inspect/select hidden or public zones and explicitly move selected cards |
| `set_card`, `apply_damage` | Per-card battle overrides and additional attack/counter damage |
| `start_match`, `set_ai`, `set_viewer` | Seed a local match, pause/resume, choose a viewing/control seat |
| `set_hand`, `give_cards`, `move_card` | Prepare cards and move loose cards among zones |
| `set_board`, `set_pokemon`, `set_turn` | Set Pokémon, attachments, damage/HP, conditions and turn fixtures |
| `dispatch` | Execute an exact legal action or required-choice command through the rules engine |
| `save_scenario`, `load_scenario`, `list_scenarios` | Save and restore named complete MatchState checkpoints |
| `load_state` | Import a complete state JSON for more complex supported fixtures |
| `undo`, `rewind_turn` | Undo a recorded command/edit or restore an earlier recorded turn |
| `finish_match`, `screenshot` | Create a result UI fixture or return a native PNG screenshot |

Seat 0 is the host/first player; seat 1 is the guest/second player. Card injections
move or replace deck cards, conserving 60 cards per seat and unique UIDs. Existing
matches can be made into unusual positions, including placing evolved Pokémon
directly, but cards must be supported Modern printings. State edits are applied to a
clone and validated before commit. A validation failure leaves the position
untouched; a presentation error can occur after commit, so inspect state before
retrying a failed request.
`load_state` permits broad changes to serializable match fields while retaining
content/rules compatibility, resolver and card-identity invariants. Unsupported
printed effects are not invented by development mode.

Snapshots include deck order, hands, Prizes, all attachments, damage, turn state,
RNG, pending choices and resolver state. Manual edits and recorded game commands
have a bounded 200-position undo history. Turn rollback starts at activation;
it cannot reconstruct turns from before development was enabled. Named files
persist independently of that history. They are match checkpoints, not copies
of player credentials, collections or wallets. Pending choices must be resolved
before incremental edits; complete checkpoint restoration is allowed while a
choice is pending. Result fixtures are not verified wins and earn no rewards.

After a tool timeout, inspect the current state before retrying an edit: a command
may have committed immediately before the connection or client timed out.

## Efficient agent workflows

Start with `get_capabilities`, then `get_session`. The compact response includes
both Active/Bench Pokémon UIDs, HP/damage, zone counts, actor, real pending-effect
status and UI/selection summaries. Use `get_card` for one card's effective stats;
reserve `get_state` for the full snapshot, private zones, resolver or bulk work.
Use `ownSeat` rather than `viewer` to identify the human: switching the view does
not swap card ownership. An in-play Pokémon UID identifies the root of its stack;
the top printed card has its own instance UID.

For deck selection, call `open_deck_picker` with the target `seat`. It returns a
`selectionId` and deck metadata, including full lists and legality. After the human
chooses, read `get_deck_selection`. `pending` means wait for the human; `cancelled`
means take no action; `selected` includes the chosen ID and `cards`. Apply those
cards with `replace_deck` using `deckCards`, especially when a multiplayer guest's
saved deck is not present in the host's profile. Replacement creates a fresh
Active/hand/Prize setup for that seat while preserving the opponent and turn.
It does not rewrite saved decks. Save a checkpoint before replacement.

For hand/deck inspection or removal, call `open_card_picker` with `seat`, `zone`,
optional `title`, and selection `minimum`/`maximum`. `maximum: 0` is inspection
only. After confirmation, `get_card_selection` returns exact `selectedUids` and
card data. Use `move_cards` with those UIDs and `zone: "lostZone"` to remove them
from the draw/hand pool while preserving sixty owned cards. A stale/missing UID
rejects the entire move. Pickers return immediately; read once after the human
responds instead of continuously polling. Shared play must be paused for pickers.

`get_state.state.players[seat].deck` is **bottom-to-top**, so the last card is the
next draw. The deck popup displays **top-first**, including an order label.
`open_menu` accepts names returned by `get_ui.menus`. Navigation keeps a
`menu-position` checkpoint; `battle` restores a suspended position. Multiplayer
navigation stays within its battle/lobby/Settings/picker routes and cannot approve
consent for either human. Deck builder navigation requires a saved custom deck ID
to open that deck; without one it opens the deck library.

## Health, attack and type overrides

Overrides belong to the individual card in the test match, including an owned
Pokémon still in a hand or deck. They travel with that instance, serialize with
checkpoints, undo and rollback, and synchronize over the approved host authority.
They never mutate the shared catalogue. `get_card` returns the effective stats.
Attack `damage` is the base printed damage; existing printed attack effects still
run when the attack is dispatched normally. `damageType` changes the damage type,
while `types` changes the Pokémon types. Weakness modifiers use a positive
`amount` with `multiplier: true` for ×N or `false` for +N. Resistance `amount` is a
nonnegative reduction. Empty weakness/resistance arrays remove those modifiers.

For example, call `set_pokemon` with:

```json
{
  "seat": 0,
  "uid": 3,
  "overrides": {
    "maxHp": 400,
    "damageType": "Lightning",
    "attacks": [{ "index": 0, "damage": 90 }],
    "weaknesses": [{ "type": "Water", "amount": 2, "multiplier": true }],
    "resistances": []
  },
  "remainingHp": 400
}
```

Use the actual UID returned by inspection. An `overrides` object replaces previous
overrides on that instance; include every override to retain, or use
`clearOverrides: true` to restore printed values. `set_card` applies the same
object to an owned card UID. Maximum HP must be positive; remaining HP and damage
must be nonnegative. Setting `remainingHp: 0` or enough total damage resolves a
real Knock Out: discard, Prizes, promotion choices, reactions and victory follow
the existing rules. A zero-health Pokémon is not left alive on the board.

`apply_damage` adds damage instead of replacing the damage total. `kind: "counters"`
places damage directly, ignoring weakness/resistance. `kind: "attack"` requires
an in-play `sourceUid`, uses battle damage calculations and accepts an optional
one-shot `damageType`. It does not dispatch a printed attack's other effects or
consume the turn; use `dispatch` for a complete legal attack.

## Verification

```powershell
npm test --prefix tools/Tcg.DevMcp
./QuickRelease-build.ps1 -OutputFolder McpSettingsAcceptance -ReportDirectory reports/mcp-settings/build -IncludeSmokeTests -DoNotSetLatest
./scripts/test-packaged-build.ps1 -OutputFolder McpSettingsAcceptance -ReportDirectory reports/mcp-settings/acceptance -Modes development,presentation,battle-ui,theme
```

The `development` smoke runs in either development or smoke-enabled production
players. It activates the actual Settings buttons without `--tcg-dev`, checks
session start/stop, connection commands, practice-only results and checkpoint
persistence, and exercises host/guest request and
approval, self-approval rejection, stale/expired commands, hidden-state gates,
guest card edits and host-seat takeover, exact checkpoint synchronization, turn
rollback, automatic gameplay history, pause and both players' revocation.
`tools/Tcg.DevMcp/verify-player.mjs <session-directory> <report-file> [adapter-path] [profile-root]` tests the
actual stdio MCP SDK connection against a running isolated player, including
invalid-state rollback and inline screenshots. Live two-machine internet Steam
testing remains a separate acceptance step.
The isolated `--tcg-smoke <report-directory> --mcp-session-smoke` bootstrap starts
a session through Settings and records its directory in `session-ready.txt`.
It uses `<report-directory>/profile`, leaves the player running for the real MCP
client, and is excluded from ordinary production builds. The optional adapter
and profile arguments verify the shipped adapter without switching the game's
profile after in-game activation.

Protocol references: [official MCP SDK](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk),
[MCP stdio transport](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/transports),
and [Codex MCP configuration](https://developers.openai.com/codex/extend/mcp).

